<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Kousséri</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1900">1900</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Kousséri was fought on 22 April 1900 near Kousséri, in present-day northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cameroon">Cameroon</a>, between French forces under Commandant <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amedee-francois-lamy">Amédée-François Lamy</a> and the army of the warlord <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rabih-az-zubayr">Rabih az-Zubayr</a>, ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bornu">Bornu</a>. The French won, and both Lamy and Rabih were killed. The victory opened the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-chad">Lake Chad</a> region to French conquest and led to the founding of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ndjamena">Fort-Lamy</a>, now N'Djamena.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-kousseri.jpg" alt="Battle of Kousséri" width="320" height="461" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Lou tresor · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mort_du_Commandant_Lamy_2.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The battle was the climax of French plans to occupy the Chari-Baguirmi region of present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chad">Chad</a>. In 1899 the Sudanese warlord <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rabih-az-zubayr">Rabih az-Zubayr</a> could field about 10,000 infantry and cavalry with rifles, most of them obsolete, besides many auxiliaries with lances or bows, and he held fortified garrisons at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baggara-arabs">Baggara</a> and Karnak Logone. Talks at Dikoa with the French representative Ferdinand de Béhagle broke down, and Béhagle was arrested. On 17 July 1899 Rabih destroyed Lieutenant Bretonnet's force at Togbao on the Chari, taking three <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannon">cannons</a>, and ordered his son Fadlallah to hang Béhagle.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> sent three columns, from Congo, from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niger-river">Niger</a> and from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a>. Émile Gentil's column from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gabon">Gabon</a>, supported by the steamboat Leon Blot, was pushed back by Rabih at Kouno at the end of 1899 but went on to Kousséri. There it met Lamy's column from Algeria and the former <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/voulet-chanoine-mission">Voulet–Chanoine Mission</a> from Niger, now under Joalland-Meynier after news of the mission's brutality had reached the European press. The columns joined on 21 April 1900, and Lamy took command.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>On 22 April the French, 700 troops with 600 riflemen and 200 cavalry from their Baguirmian allies, left Kousséri in three columns and attacked Rabih's camp. Lamy was killed in the fighting, but Rabih's forces were overwhelmed. As Rabih tried to flee across the Chari he was shot in the head by a skirmisher, who, hearing of a bonus for the body, went back and brought in his head and right hand. The French lost 28 dead and 75 wounded; Rabih's side lost 1,000 to 1,500 dead and more than 3,000 wounded, among them women and children who accompanied the army.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The victory secured French control over most of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chad">Chad</a>, which became part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-colonial-empire">French colonial empire</a>, and linked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>'s three most stable possessions in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senegal">Senegal</a> and the Upper Niger valley, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gabon">Gabon</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/republic-of-the-congo">Congo-Brazzaville</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a>. The marches of the columns helped define the practical borders between French and British rule. French colonial histories usually treat Kousséri as the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scramble-for-africa">Scramble for Africa</a> and the beginning of the pacification phase of French West and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-equatorial-africa">Equatorial Africa</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Kousséri” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The French advance into the region and the French conquest of Bornu, which culminated in Rabih's defeat and death at the <b>Battle of Kousséri</b> in April 1900, created a direct confrontation with Wadai.</blockquote><a href="/empire/wadai-empire">Wadai Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
